      <h2 style="color: #ffffff; font-size: 1.7rem; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.35; margin-bottom: 18px;">Destination Handover, Odometer Sign-Off &amp; Joint Condition Inspection for Bike Transportation In Muddebihal</h2>
      <div style="font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.8; color: #cbd5e1;"><h3>Destination Delivery Handover Procedure for Two‑Wheeler & Motorcycle</h3>
<p style="font-size: 1.04rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #f1f5f9; font-weight: 500; margin-bottom: 12px;">When a two‑wheeler or motorcycle reaches its final destination, a structured handover ensures that the vehicle is received in the exact condition documented at pickup.</p>
<ul style="list-style: none; padding-left: 0; margin: 16px 0 20px 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px;"><li style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 10px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07); padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 0.92rem; line-height: 1.55; color: #cbd5e1;"><i class="fas fa-check-circle" style="color: #10b981; margin-top: 4px; flex-shrink: 0; font-size: 0.9rem;"></i><span>The process revolves around three core actions: a daylight condition re‑inspection compared with the original pickup photographs, an odometer verification against the LR (Lorry Receipt) bilty, and a signed condition sign‑off by the recipient.</span></li></ul>
<p>Following these steps creates a transparent record, minimizes disputes, and protects both the transporter and the buyer.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daylight Condition Re‑inspection</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>Conduct the inspection under natural daylight to reveal any surface defects, scratches, dents, or paint inconsistencies that artificial lighting might conceal.</li>
        <li>Use the pickup photographs as a reference baseline; compare each angle, wheel, and body panel side‑by‑side.</li>
        <li>Document any new findings with timestamped photos, noting the exact location on the vehicle (e.g., “left rear fender scratch, 2 cm long”).</li>
        <li>If discrepancies are identified, record them on the handover checklist and discuss them immediately with the driver and the receiving party.</li>
    </ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Odometer Verification Against LR Bilty</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>Read the odometer directly from the instrument cluster, ensuring the vehicle is stationary and the engine is off to avoid false readings.</li>
        <li>Cross‑check this reading with the mileage recorded on the LR bilty, which was logged at the point of origin.</li>
        <li>Note any variance greater than 5 km; such differences may indicate tampering, mis‑reading, or an error in the original documentation.</li>
        <li>Both the transporter’s representative and the recipient should initial the mileage verification line on the handover form.</li>
    </ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Signed Condition Sign‑off</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>After the visual and mileage checks, the recipient reviews the completed checklist and accompanying photographs.</li>
        <li>The recipient signs the condition acknowledgment, confirming that the vehicle’s state matches the documented condition or noting any agreed‑upon exceptions.</li>
        <li>A copy of the signed form, along with the inspection photos, is provided to the recipient, while the original remains with the logistics provider for future reference.</li>
        <li>Both parties retain a duplicate of the signed document to support any warranty claims or insurance processes.</li>
    </ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>By rigorously applying this three‑step handover—daylight re‑inspection, odometer verification, and signed sign‑off—stakeholders ensure accountability, preserve the vehicle’s documented history, and foster trust throughout the delivery chain.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


